      "topic_id": "01effa1f-ca03-4e48-9596-561c764c038b",
      "text": "What is the primary purpose of specifying a uniformity ratio (UR) of 0.6 minimum in general occupied spaces per IES standards?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "To ensure 60% of fixtures operate at rated capacity, optimizing energy efficiency and lighting performance"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "To guarantee average illumination across the space is at least 60% of peak illumination, providing consistent lighting conditions"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "To prevent dark zones by ensuring minimum illumination is at least 60% of average illumination"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "To limit energy consumption to 60% of the building's total lighting budget, promoting sustainable design practices"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "c",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "UR 0.6 means the darkest point must receive at least 60% of average illumination, preventing dark zones and reducing eye strain. This prevents excessive brightness variation across a space. [A]: Fixture capacity and operating levels are independent of uniformity ratio; UR addresses spatial consistency, not individual fixture output. [B]: This inverts the definition; UR compares minimum to average, not average to peak. Peak illumination (brightest points) may far exceed average. [D]: Uniformity ratio is a spatial quality metric, not an energy budget allocation; energy consumption is managed through lighting power density (LPD) limits.",
      "explanation": null,
      "code_citation": "Article: Illumination Levels by Occupancy and Task, Section: Uniformity Ratio",
